<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Brescia</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1337 CE (approx.) · capital Brescia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A combative Lombard city-republic, home of the reformer Arnold of Brescia, that withstood a siege by Emperor Frederick II in 1238.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia's</a> citizens took power from their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in the late 11th and early 12th centuries, and the commune was governed by consuls by around 1100. It controlled a rich territory of valleys, iron mines and lakes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-garda">Lake Garda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-iseo">Lake Iseo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The commune was run by consuls and later a podestà. The preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-of-brescia">Arnold of Brescia</a>, who attacked clerical wealth, came from the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lombard-league">Lombard League</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, resisted a long siege by Frederick II in 1238 and another by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry VII</a> in 1311. It was famed for iron working and arms making, and its Old Cathedral, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-cathedral-brescia">Rotonda</a>, dates from this era.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Exhausted by faction, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> lords of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-verona">Verona</a> and then in 1337 submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azzone-visconti">Azzone Visconti</a>, joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-milan">Lordship of Milan</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice</a> took it in 1426.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1100 CE – 